# The kernel options Cilium needs, on top of configs/k4s-tiny.config.
#
# This is not applied to the node image. It exists to price the change: build
# with it, measure, and then decide. docs/cilium.md has the measured numbers and
# the command to reproduce them.
#
# The lists are Cilium's own system requirements for the version being
# evaluated (v1.20.2). Everything it documents as required for the features a
# normal deployment uses is here; the optional features that are off by default
# are not, and are listed at the end.

# --- the gates ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Cilium's list cannot simply be appended to tinyconfig: several of its options
# are unreachable until the subsystem they live in is switched on, and two of the
# symbols it names are not settable any more.
#
#   * NET_SCHED hides NET_CLS_ACT, NET_SCH_INGRESS and NET_CLS_BPF. It is a
#     menuconfig, so with it off those three are invisible and appending them
#     does nothing.
#   * DEBUG_INFO is not a settable symbol now: it is derived from the "Debug
#     information" choice, so DEBUG_INFO_BTF needs a DWARF choice turned on and
#     DEBUG_INFO_NONE turned off, not DEBUG_INFO=y.
#   * the filter and nat tables moved behind IP_NF_IPTABLES_LEGACY, which needs
#     NETFILTER_XTABLES_LEGACY in turn. IP_NF_IPTABLES=y on its own gives an
#     iptables with no tables in it.
#   * CRYPTO_SHA1 needs CRYPTO, and FIB_RULES needs IP_ADVANCED_ROUTER. Both are
#     menuconfigs.
#   * BPF_EVENTS needs KPROBE_EVENTS (or UPROBE_EVENTS) and PERF_EVENTS, and
#     KPROBE_EVENTS sits inside `if FTRACE`, so it needs FTRACE and with it the
#     tracing subsystem.
#   * FIB_RULES has no prompt at all: it is selected by IP_MULTIPLE_TABLES, so
#     setting it directly does nothing. Cilium's own documentation warns about
#     this one.
#   * TPROXY lives in the mangle table and needs IP_NF_MANGLE; the CT target
#     needs IP_NF_RAW.
#
# None of this is in Cilium's documentation, because distribution kernels have
# all of it already. On a tinyconfig kernel it is most of the work.
CONFIG_NET_SCHED=y
CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO_DWARF_TOOLCHAIN_DEFAULT=y
# CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO_NONE is not set
CONFIG_CRYPTO=y
CONFIG_IP_ADVANCED_ROUTER=y
CONFIG_KPROBES=y
CONFIG_FTRACE=y
CONFIG_KPROBE_EVENTS=y
CONFIG_IP_MULTIPLE_TABLES=y
CONFIG_NETFILTER_XTABLES_LEGACY=y
CONFIG_IP_NF_IPTABLES_LEGACY=y
CONFIG_IP_NF_MANGLE=y
CONFIG_IP_NF_RAW=y

# --- the eBPF datapath: Cilium's base requirements ---------------------------
# These are what let Cilium attach anything at all. Its datapath is a tc/eBPF
# classifier, so without NET_CLS_BPF and NET_SCH_INGRESS it cannot attach a
# single program.
#
# NET_ACT_BPF is not in Cilium's list either: it is the action half of the same
# machinery.
CONFIG_NET_CLS_ACT=y
CONFIG_NET_CLS_BPF=y
CONFIG_NET_SCH_INGRESS=y
CONFIG_NET_ACT_BPF=y
CONFIG_BPF_EVENTS=y
CONFIG_SCHEDSTATS=y
CONFIG_CRYPTO_SHA1=y

# --- BTF --------------------------------------------------------------------
# A base requirement since Cilium 1.14: its programs are compiled with CO-RE and
# read kernel types out of BTF. Generating BTF needs DWARF in vmlinux and pahole
# (>= 1.22) on the build host, so the gate above turns on a debug-info build.
# Neither is free: this is the expensive half of the fragment.
CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO_BTF=y

# --- tunneling and routing --------------------------------------------------
# VXLAN is Cilium's default for pod-to-pod traffic across nodes. FIB_RULES comes
# with IP_MULTIPLE_TABLES above; it is listed here only to say why it matters.
CONFIG_VXLAN=y
CONFIG_GENEVE=y
CONFIG_FIB_RULES=y

# --- masquerading, in its default (iptables) mode ---------------------------
# Cilium defaults to enable-bpf-masquerade=false, so iptables does the
# masquerading from inside the agent's container. That needs the set and match
# modules it lists, plus the tables and target a distribution kernel would
# already have: this kernel has neither the filter nor the nat table, so
# iptables cannot even create its standard chains today.
CONFIG_NETFILTER_XT_SET=y
CONFIG_IP_SET=y
CONFIG_IP_SET_HASH_IP=y
CONFIG_NETFILTER_XT_MATCH_COMMENT=y
CONFIG_IP_NF_FILTER=y
CONFIG_IP_NF_NAT=y
CONFIG_NETFILTER_XT_TARGET_MASQUERADE=y

# --- L7 and FQDN policies ---------------------------------------------------
# Redirection to the L7 proxy uses TPROXY.
CONFIG_NETFILTER_XT_TARGET_TPROXY=y
CONFIG_NETFILTER_XT_TARGET_MARK=y
CONFIG_NETFILTER_XT_TARGET_CT=y
CONFIG_NETFILTER_XT_MATCH_MARK=y
CONFIG_NETFILTER_XT_MATCH_SOCKET=y

# --- netlink families the Cilium agent opens unconditionally ------------------
# XFRM looks like it is only needed for IPsec, and this fragment originally left
# it out on exactly that reasoning. It is not optional. vishvananda/netlink's
# NewHandle opens sockets for NETLINK_ROUTE, NETLINK_XFRM and NETLINK_NETFILTER,
# and the first failure aborts the whole handle:
#
#   var SupportedNlFamilies = []int{unix.NETLINK_ROUTE, unix.NETLINK_XFRM, unix.NETLINK_NETFILTER}
#
# The agent calls it on startup, from the route reconciler's OnStart
# (pkg/datapath/linux/route/reconciler/reconciler.go:76), so without NETLINK_XFRM
# it dies with "protocol not supported" -- EPROTONOSUPPORT straight out of
# socket(2) -- before it touches a single route. CONFIG_XFRM_USER is what
# registers the family; it selects XFRM.
CONFIG_XFRM_USER=y

# --- deliberately not included ----------------------------------------------
# Each is off by default and each costs more, so they are left out of the price
# until something needs them:
#   IPsec *payload* (INET_ESP, INET_AH, CRYPTO_GCM, ...) -- note that the XFRM
#   netlink family itself is required and is set above; only the ESP/AH
#   transforms are still left out. Also WireGuard, netkit device mode, XDP
#   sockets, BPF LSM, time namespaces.
